# ── KUBERNETES SECRETS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Intent: block Kubernetes Secret manifests (my-secret.yaml and friends). The
# pattern is repo-wide, though, so it catches any file whose name merely contains
# "secret" -- which is a trap rather than a guard for one directory in
# particular.
*secret*.yaml
*secret*.yml
!*secret*.example.yaml
!*secret*.example.yml

# GitHub Actions workflows are NEVER Kubernetes manifests, and a gitignored
# workflow does not merely fail to be committed -- IT SILENTLY DOES NOT EXIST.
# GitHub runs workflows from the committed tree, so an ignored one never fires
# and nothing reports that it is missing.
#
# This was not hypothetical: `.github/workflows/08-secret-history-audit.yml`, the
# scheduled job that watches for live credentials in git history, matched
# `*secret*.yml` and was refused by `git add` with only a hint. Had that been
# forced past with `-f` or not noticed, SEC-01's whole point -- that nothing was
# watching -- would have been "fixed" by a file that was never in the repository.
#
# Narrow by design: this re-includes workflow definitions only, which must be
# committed to function at all. It does not re-include anything elsewhere.
!.github/workflows/*secret*.yaml
!.github/workflows/*secret*.yml

